export const STATIC_BODIES = {
 "/tutorials": "<main><h2>What You Will Learn</h2><ul><li>How to calculate CGPA in Nigerian universities</li><li>Understanding WAEC grading system</li><li>JAMB score calculation and admission tips</li><li>Effective study techniques</li></ul><p>Explore a wide range of educational tutorials designed for Nigerian students. Learn how to calculate CGPA, understand WAEC grading system, improve your JAMB score, and discover effective study strategies to excel academically.</p><p>Use our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator alongside these tutorials.</p></main>",
 "/cgpa-calculator": "<main><h1>Advanced CGPA Calculator</h1><p>Calculate your university CGPA using Nigerian grading system</p><h3>Total Units</h3><h3>Your CGPA</h3><h2>What is CGPA?</h2><p>CGPA stands for Cumulative Grade Point Average. It is the figure Nigerian universities use to summarize a student's overall academic performance across every semester completed so far, from 100 level up to final year. Unlike a single semester's GPA, which resets each term, your CGPA carries forward and updates every time new results are released — it's the number that ends up printed on your transcript and certificate.</p><h2>GPA vs CGPA — what's the difference?</h2><p>GPA (Grade Point Average) covers one semester only. CGPA (Cumulative Grade Point Average) is the running average across all the semesters you've completed. A bad GPA in one semester will pull your CGPA down, but because CGPA is cumulative, a single weak semester matters less as more semesters — and more total credit units — are added to the average.</p><h2>How CGPA is calculated in Nigeria</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities calculate CGPA using this formula:</p><p>CGPA = (Sum of Grade Point × Credit Unit for every course) ÷ (Sum of all Credit Units)</p><p>In practice: for each course, multiply the credit unit (e.g. 2, 3, or 4 units) by the grade point earned (A=5, B=4, C=3, D=2, E=1, F=0). Add up those totals for every course you've ever taken, then divide by the total number of credit units. That single number is your CGPA. This calculator performs that exact calculation for you — just enter each course's unit and grade above.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Say a student took four courses in a semester:</p><ul><li>MTH101 (3 units) — grade A (5 points) → 15 grade points</li><li>PHY101 (3 units) — grade B (4 points) → 12 grade points</li><li>CHM101 (2 units) — grade C (3 points) → 6 grade points</li><li>GST101 (2 units) — grade B (4 points) → 8 grade points</li></ul><p>Total grade points = 15 + 12 + 6 + 8 = 41. Total units = 3 + 3 + 2 + 2 = 10. GPA for that semester = 41 ÷ 10 = 4.10. To get your CGPA, you carry this same running total (grade points and units) into the next semester instead of starting over.</p><h2>The Nigerian university grading scale</h2><p>This is the 5-point scale used by the calculator above, and by most (though not all) Nigerian universities:</p><ul><li>A = 5 points (70–100, Excellent)</li><li>B = 4 points (60–69, Very Good)</li><li>C = 3 points (50–59, Good)</li><li>D = 2 points (45–49, Fair)</li><li>E = 1 point (40–44, Pass)</li><li>F = 0 points (Below 40, Fail)</li></ul><p>Note: exact score-to-grade boundaries and the number of points per grade can differ slightly between universities, and some polytechnics and colleges of education use a 4-point scale instead of this 5-point one. Always confirm the exact scale in your own institution's academic handbook.</p><h2>How your CGPA maps to a degree class</h2><p>At graduation, your final CGPA is converted into a degree classification. Use our GPA Class Calculator to see exactly which class your CGPA falls into, along with the typical CGPA ranges for First Class, Second Class Upper, Second Class Lower, Third Class, and Pass.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Explore more tools: You can also check our WAEC Calculator and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/waec-grade-calculator": "<main><h1>WAEC Grade Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Convert WAEC scores into official grades</p><h2>Results</h2><h2>What is the WAEC grading system?</h2><p>WAEC (West African Examinations Council) conducts the WASSCE (West African Senior School Certificate Examination), the exam most Nigerian students sit at the end of senior secondary school. For every subject, your raw score out of 100 is converted into a letter grade from A1 (Excellent) down to F9 (Fail). Universities, polytechnics, and colleges of education use these grades — not the raw scores — to decide admission eligibility.</p><h2>WAEC grade breakdown</h2><ul><li>A1: 75 – 100 (Excellent)</li><li>B2: 70 – 74 (Very Good)</li><li>B3: 65 – 69 (Good)</li><li>C4: 60 – 64 (Credit)</li><li>C5: 55 – 59 (Credit)</li><li>C6: 50 – 54 (Credit)</li><li>D7: 45 – 49 (Pass)</li><li>E8: 40 – 44 (Pass)</li><li>F9: 0 – 39 (Fail)</li></ul><h2>Why the \"credit\" grades matter most</h2><p>Grades A1 through C6 are all classified as credit passes. This distinction matters because most Nigerian tertiary institutions require a minimum number of credit passes — commonly five subjects, almost always including English Language and Mathematics — before you're even eligible to apply for admission, regardless of your JAMB score. D7 and E8 are passes, but they don't count as credits. The exact number of credits and which other subjects must be among them is set by each institution and course, so always confirm the specific requirement for the course you're applying to.</p><h2>How to use this calculator</h2><p>Enter each subject name and the raw score you have (or expect) for it, then press Calculate Grades. The tool converts every score into its WAEC letter grade instantly using the scale above, so you can quickly see which of your results are credit passes.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>A student scores 78 in Mathematics, 62 in English Language, and 51 in Biology. Using the scale above: 78 converts to A1, 62 converts to C4, and 51 converts to C6. All three are credit passes.</p><h2>WAEC vs NECO</h2><p>NECO (National Examinations Council) is Nigeria's other major school-leaving exam body, and it uses a very similar A1–F9 grading scale. Most institutions treat WAEC and NECO credit passes as equivalent, but always check your target institution's specific admission requirements.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check your JAMB Score.</p></main>",
 "/jamb-score-calculator": "<main><h1>JAMB Score Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Enter your UTME subject scores to calculate total</p><h2>What is a JAMB score?</h2><p>JAMB (Joint Admissions and Matriculation Board) administers the UTME (Unified Tertiary Matriculation Examination) — the entrance exam every Nigerian student sits before applying to universities, polytechnics, and colleges of education. Your \"JAMB score\" is the total of your marks across four subjects, out of a maximum possible 400.</p><h2>How the JAMB score is calculated</h2><p>Every candidate sits English Language (compulsory for all courses) plus three other subjects relevant to the course they're applying for — for example, a Physics/Chemistry/Biology combination for Medicine, or Government/Economics/Literature for Mass Communication. Each subject is scored out of 100, and the four scores are simply added together:</p><p>JAMB Total = English score + Subject 2 score + Subject 3 score + Subject 4 score</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>If you score 70 in English, 65 in Mathematics, 60 in Physics, and 55 in Chemistry:</p><p>Total = 70 + 65 + 60 + 55 = 250</p><h2>What counts as a good JAMB score?</h2><p>There's no single official \"good score\" — it depends on your course and target institution's cut-off mark for that admission cycle. The result label this calculator shows you (from \"Excellent\" down to \"Low Admission Chances\") is based on these informal bands, not an official JAMB publication:</p><ul><li>300+ → Excellent, competitive for most courses</li><li>250–299 → Very competitive for most courses</li><li>200–249 → Reasonable for many courses</li><li>180–199 → Around the general minimum range for some institutions</li><li>Below 180 → Likely below JAMB's general minimum for most courses</li></ul><p>Always confirm your target course's actual departmental cut-off mark with the institution, and JAMB's official minimum for the current admission cycle on jamb.gov.ng — both can sit well above (or below) these general ranges, and both change every cycle.</p><h2>After JAMB: what else affects admission?</h2><p>Your UTME score alone rarely decides admission outright. Most institutions also run a Post-UTME screening exercise, and some factor in your WAEC/NECO O'Level grades alongside your JAMB score before making a final offer.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check the WAEC grading system.</p></main>",
 "/gpa-class-calculator": "<main><h1>GPA Class Calculator Nigeria</h1><p>Enter your CGPA to know your degree classification</p><h3>Nigerian Degree Classification</h3><p>This is the most common scale on Nigerian universities' 5-point system — some institutions use 4.00, not 4.50, as the First Class boundary. See \"A note on variation between institutions\" further down this page.</p><h2>What is degree classification in Nigeria?</h2><p>Degree classification is the honours label — First Class, Second Class Upper, and so on — printed alongside your final CGPA on your degree certificate when you graduate from a Nigerian university. It's a single-tier summary of everything your CGPA already represents: your cumulative academic performance across every semester of your program.</p><h2>How CGPA converts to degree class</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities use the 5-point CGPA scale shown in the table above to place graduating students into one of five classes. Your CGPA is calculated the same way throughout your program — see our CGPA Calculator for the full formula — and at graduation, your final cumulative figure is simply matched against the class ranges above.</p><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>A final CGPA of 4.70 falls in the 4.50–5.00 range, so that graduate receives a First Class degree. A final CGPA of 3.80 falls in the 3.50–4.49 range, so that graduate receives a Second Class Upper. A final CGPA of 2.90 falls in the 2.40–3.49 range — Second Class Lower.</p><h2>Why degree classification matters</h2><ul><li>Many employers use it as an initial screening criterion for graduate roles</li><li>Most postgraduate programs (Master's, PGD) set a minimum class or CGPA for admission</li><li>Some scholarships, fellowships, and NYSC-linked opportunities favour First Class and Second Class Upper graduates</li></ul><h2>A note on variation between institutions</h2><p>The ranges above reflect the most common convention on a 5-point scale, but they are not a single fixed national law — each university's senate sets its own exact boundaries, and a small number of institutions use a 4-point scale with different cut-offs entirely. If you're close to a class boundary, confirm the precise figures in your own institution's academic regulations.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>You can also calculate your CGPA or check your JAMB Score.</p></main>",
 "/age-calculator": "<main><h1>Age Calculator</h1><p>Enter a date of birth to get the exact age in years, months and days, plus total days lived and a countdown to the next birthday.</p><h2>Check an age limit</h2><p>Find out if you meet the minimum or maximum age for a scholarship, admission, job or competition on its cutoff date. Fill in at least one of the two limits.</p><h2>Track many birthdays at once</h2><p>Keep a list of family, friends or classmates and see everyone's age and upcoming birthdays in one place with the Age Tracker.</p><h2>How this age calculator works</h2><p>Your exact age is counted the same way you would do it on paper. First the calculator counts the whole years between your date of birth and the chosen date. Next it counts the whole months since your last birthday. Whatever is left over is the number of days. The totals in months, weeks, days and hours are worked out from the same two dates, and every leap year in between is included automatically.</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>Suppose a student was born on Monday, 15 August 2005, and wants to know their age on 26 October 2026.</p><ul><li>Whole years: from 15 August 2005 to 15 August 2026 is 21 years.</li><li>Whole months: from 15 August 2026 to 15 October 2026 is 2 months.</li><li>Remaining days: from 15 October 2026 to 26 October 2026 is 11 days.</li></ul><p>So the student is 21 years, 2 months and 11 days old. That is 7,742 days in total, which is exactly 1,106 weeks, and their next birthday is 293 days away on 15 August 2027.</p><h2>Uses for an exact age</h2><ul><li>Checking the age requirement on a scholarship, fellowship or competition form.</li><li>Confirming the age you will be on an admission or registration cutoff date.</li><li>Filling in forms that ask for age in years and months, such as school records.</li><li>Planning ahead for a milestone birthday or counting down to the next one.</li></ul><h2>Age limits Nigerian students often meet</h2><p>Many opportunities set an age limit and measure it on a fixed date, not on the day you apply. Scholarships and graduate programmes often have a maximum age, while school and university admission have a minimum age. For NYSC, graduates who are over 30 years old at graduation are exempted from service and receive an exemption certificate instead. Age rules change from time to time, so always confirm the current figure with the organisation, then use the age limit checker above with their exact cutoff date.</p><h2>Leap year birthdays</h2><p>A person born on 29 February has a real birthday only in leap years, such as 2024 and 2028. In the other years this calculator places the birthday on 28 February. Day counts are never affected, because every real calendar day between the two dates is counted.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>More free tools: the Age Tracker, the CGPA Calculator and the JAMB Score Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/age-tracker": "<main><h1>Age Tracker</h1><p>Save the birthdays of family, friends or students and always see their exact age, who has a birthday coming up and the next milestone.</p><h2>Birthdays in the next 30 days</h2><h2>Backup and calendar</h2><p>Your list is saved only in this browser on this device. Export a backup to move it to another phone or computer, or add every birthday to Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or Outlook as a yearly event.</p><h2>What the age tracker does</h2><p>The age tracker keeps a list of people and their dates of birth and works out everything else for you each time you open it. For every person you see their exact age in years, months and days, how many days they have lived, how many days remain until their next birthday and how old they will turn. The list can be sorted by next birthday, name or age, and searched by name or group.</p><h2>Your data stays on your device</h2><p>There is no sign-up. Names and birthdays are stored only in your own browser, using its local storage, and are never sent to our server. The flip side is that clearing your browser data removes the list, and it does not sync between devices by itself. Use the Export backup button to save a copy, then Import backup on another device to bring the same list across.</p><h2>Who it is useful for</h2><ul><li>Class representatives and course reps keeping track of classmates' birthdays.</li><li>Teachers and school administrators who need pupils' ages for records or class placement.</li><li>Parents tracking the ages of their children for school entry and exams.</li><li>Anyone who wants a private birthday reminder list for family and friends.</li></ul><h2>Milestones and the live age counter</h2><p>Besides birthdays, the tracker shows each person's next day-count milestone, such as 8,000 or 10,000 days old, which makes a fun reason to celebrate. Press Live age on anyone to watch their age tick upward in years and seconds. Because only the date of birth is saved, the live count starts from midnight on the day they were born.</p><h2>Birthday calendar</h2><p>The Download birthday calendar button creates a standard calendar file with one yearly event per person. Open it with Google Calendar, Apple Calendar or Outlook and every birthday repeats automatically each year, with reminders handled by your calendar app. People born on 29 February get their event on the last day of February in common years.</p><p>Need a single age instead? Use the Age Calculator to find an exact age on any date or check an age limit. You can also plan exams with the Study Planner.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools": "<main><h1>Engineering Tools</h1><p>Free electronics calculators for engineering and physics students, hobbyists and technicians. Identify components and work out circuit values in seconds.</p><h2>Identify electronic components</h2><p>Most small components do not have their value printed in full. Through-hole resistors use coloured bands, surface-mount resistors use short number and letter codes, and ceramic capacitors use a three-digit code in picofarads. The Resistor Colour Code Calculator, the SMD Resistor Code Calculator and the Capacitor Code Calculator turn these markings into values, and also work the other way round when you need to find a part.</p><h2>Design and analyse circuits</h2><p>Once you know your parts, the circuit calculators do the arithmetic. Use the Ohm's Law Calculator for voltage, current, resistance and power, the Series and Parallel Calculator to combine components, the Voltage Divider Calculator for reference voltages, the LED Resistor Calculator to protect LEDs, and the 555 Timer Calculator for oscillators and delays.</p><h2>Built for students</h2><p>Every tool explains the formula it uses and includes worked examples, so you can check your own working for practicals, assignments and exams in physics and electrical or electronic engineering. Values can be typed the way engineers write them, such as 4k7 for 4.7 kΩ, 100n for 100 nF or 20mA for 0.02 A, and results use standard component values from the E12, E24 and E96 series where it matters.</p><ul><li>Colour codes follow IEC 60062 and preferred values follow IEC 60063.</li><li>Everything runs in your browser, so the tools work even on a slow connection once the page has loaded.</li><li>All tools are free, with no sign-up.</li></ul></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/resistor-color-code": "<main><h1>Resistor Colour Code Calculator</h1><p>Tap the colours on your resistor to see its value, tolerance and range, or type a value to find the colours. Works for 3, 4, 5 and 6-band resistors and for colour-coded inductors.</p><p>Grey is ±0.01% under IEC 60062:2016; some older parts use grey for ±0.05%.</p><p>A single black band marks a zero-ohm link (a jumper).</p><h2>Find the colours for a value</h2><p>Note: this is not a standard E-series value, so it may be hard to buy.</p><h2>How to read a resistor colour code</h2><p>Resistors are too small to print numbers on, so their value is shown with coloured bands. Each colour stands for a number from 0 to 9. Hold the resistor so the bands that are bunched together are on the left. The band standing on its own on the right, usually gold or silver, is the tolerance band.</p><ul><li>Read the first bands as digits. A 4-band resistor has two digit bands and a 5-band resistor has three.</li><li>The next band is the multiplier: the number of zeros to add, or gold for divide by 10 and silver for divide by 100.</li><li>The last band is the tolerance: how far the real value may be from the marked value.</li><li>On a 6-band resistor the extra band at the end is the temperature coefficient in ppm per kelvin.</li></ul><h2>Resistor colour code chart</h2><p>Values follow the international standard IEC 60062:2016. A popular way to remember the digit order is the phrase BB ROY of Great Britain had a Very Good Wife: Black, Brown, Red, Orange, Yellow, Green, Blue, Violet, Grey, White.</p><h2>Worked examples</h2><h3>4-band: yellow, violet, orange, gold</h3><p>Yellow is 4 and violet is 7, so the digits are 47. Orange is a multiplier of 1,000, giving 47,000 Ω or 47 kΩ. Gold means ±5%, so the real resistance lies between 44.65 kΩ and 49.35 kΩ.</p><h3>5-band: brown, black, black, brown, brown</h3><p>The three digits are 1, 0 and 0, which is 100. The brown multiplier is ×10, giving 1,000 Ω or 1 kΩ. The final brown band is a tolerance of ±1%, so the value is between 990 Ω and 1,010 Ω.</p><h3>3-band: red, red, brown</h3><p>The digits are 22 and brown multiplies by 10, so the value is 220 Ω. With no tolerance band the tolerance is ±20%.</p><h2>Standard resistor values (E-series)</h2><p>Resistors are made in preferred values called the E-series, defined in IEC 60063. The E12 series has 12 values in each decade (10, 12, 15, 18, 22, 27, 33, 39, 47, 56, 68, 82) and suits ±10% parts, E24 suits ±5% parts and E96 suits ±1% parts. This calculator tells you when a reading is not a standard value, which usually means a colour was misread or the resistor was read from the wrong end.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>More tools: SMD Resistor Code, Ohm's Law Calculator and Series and Parallel Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/smd-resistor-code": "<main><h1>SMD Resistor Code Calculator</h1><p>Type the marking printed on a surface-mount resistor to get its value. Handles 3-digit, 4-digit, R-notation and EIA-96 codes.</p><p>This marking can be read more than one way. A trailing R is the decimal point on ordinary parts but a ×0.01 multiplier on 1% EIA-96 parts. Measure the resistor with a multimeter, or check the size of the part (EIA-96 codes are used on 1% parts).</p><p>Tolerances shown are the usual ones for this marking system; the code itself does not state the tolerance.</p><h2>Find the SMD code for a value</h2><h2>EIA-96 code table</h2><h2>How SMD resistor codes work</h2><p>Surface-mount (SMD) resistors are too small for colour bands, so their value is printed as a short code. Very small parts, such as the 0402 size and smaller, often have no marking at all and must be measured. There are four common systems.</p><h3>3-digit code</h3><p>Used on standard parts, usually ±5%. The first two digits are the significant figures and the third digit is the number of zeros. For example, 472 is 47 followed by two zeros, which is 4,700 Ω or 4.7 kΩ. A code of 100 is 10 Ω, not 100 Ω.</p><h3>4-digit code</h3><p>Used on precision parts, usually ±1%. The first three digits are the significant figures and the fourth is the number of zeros. For example, 1002 is 100 followed by two zeros, which is 10,000 Ω or 10 kΩ, and 4751 is 4,750 Ω.</p><h3>R notation for small values</h3><p>Values below 10 Ω cannot be written with the digit codes, so the letter R is used as the decimal point. 4R7 is 4.7 Ω, R10 is 0.1 Ω and 0R22 is 0.22 Ω. Current-sense resistors often use this marking.</p><h3>EIA-96 code</h3><p>Small 1% resistors use a three-character code: two digits and a letter. The digits are a position in the E96 list of preferred values (01 = 100, 02 = 102 and so on up to 96 = 976), and the letter is the multiplier. For example, 68X is 499 × 0.1 = 49.9 Ω and 01C is 100 × 100 = 10 kΩ. Use the table above to look up any code.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Reading through-hole parts instead? Use the Resistor Colour Code Calculator or the Capacitor Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/capacitor-code": "<main><h1>Capacitor Code Calculator</h1><p>Enter the code printed on a ceramic, film or tantalum capacitor to get its value in pF, nF and µF, plus its tolerance and voltage rating.</p><p>A 9 in the third place means ×0.1 (for values under 10 pF).</p><p>An 8 in the third place means ×0.01.</p><h2>Find the code for a value</h2><p>This value can't be written as a 3-digit code. Codes have two significant figures and run from about 1 pF to 99 µF; larger capacitors (electrolytics) print the value directly.</p><h2>Common capacitor codes</h2><h2>How to read capacitor codes</h2><p>Small ceramic and film capacitors print a three-digit code instead of their full value. The code is always in picofarads (pF). The first two digits are the significant figures and the third digit is the number of zeros to add.</p><ul><li>104 is 10 followed by four zeros: 100,000 pF, which is 100 nF or 0.1 µF.</li><li>222 is 22 followed by two zeros: 2,200 pF, which is 2.2 nF.</li><li>470 is 47 with no zeros: 47 pF. A code of 47 on its own also means 47 pF.</li><li>For values under 10 pF the letter R marks the decimal point, so 4R7 is 4.7 pF.</li></ul><p>European-style markings put the unit letter where the decimal point would be. 2n2 is 2.2 nF, n47 is 0.47 nF (470 pF), 4u7 is 4.7 µF and p68 is 0.68 pF. Large electrolytic capacitors simply print the value and voltage, for example 470 µF 25 V, and must be connected the right way round.</p><h2>Tolerance letters</h2><p>Letters B, C and D give an absolute tolerance in picofarads and are used on small capacitors below about 10 pF.</p><h2>Voltage codes</h2><p>Some capacitors carry an EIA voltage code made of a digit and a letter. Common ones are 0J for 6.3 V, 1A for 10 V, 1C for 16 V, 1E for 25 V, 1V for 35 V, 1H for 50 V, 2A for 100 V, 2E for 250 V and 2J for 630 V. Always choose a capacitor rated comfortably above the highest voltage in your circuit.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Combining capacitors? Use the Series and Parallel Calculator, or time a circuit with the 555 Timer Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/ohms-law-calculator": "<main><h1>Ohm's Law Calculator</h1><p>Fill in any two of voltage, current, resistance and power. The other two are calculated instantly.</p><h2>Ohm's law formulas</h2><p>Ohm's law links voltage (V), current (I) and resistance (R): V = I × R. Electrical power (P) is the rate energy is used: P = V × I. Putting the two together gives every formula you need from any two known values.</p><ul><li>Voltage: V = I × R, V = P ÷ I, or V = √(P × R)</li><li>Current: I = V ÷ R, I = P ÷ V, or I = √(P ÷ R)</li><li>Resistance: R = V ÷ I, R = V² ÷ P, or R = P ÷ I²</li><li>Power: P = V × I, P = I² × R, or P = V² ÷ R</li></ul><h2>Worked examples</h2><h3>Current through a resistor</h3><p>A 220 Ω resistor is connected across a 12 V supply. The current is I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 220 = 0.0545 A, or about 54.5 mA. The power is P = V² ÷ R = 144 ÷ 220 = 0.655 W, so a 1 W resistor is needed; a common 0.25 W resistor would overheat.</p><h3>Resistance of a heating element</h3><p>A 1,000 W electric kettle runs on 230 V mains. Its current is I = P ÷ V = 1000 ÷ 230 = 4.35 A, and its element resistance is R = V² ÷ P = 52,900 ÷ 1000 = 52.9 Ω.</p><h2>Units and prefixes</h2><p>Voltage is measured in volts, current in amperes (amps), resistance in ohms and power in watts. In electronics small currents are given in milliamps (1 mA = 0.001 A) and large resistances in kilohms (1 kΩ = 1,000 Ω) or megohms (1 MΩ = 1,000,000 Ω). You can type these prefixes directly into the calculator.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Voltage Divider Calculator, LED Resistor Calculator and Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/series-parallel-calculator": "<main><h1>Series and Parallel Circuit Calculator</h1><p>Paste a series or parallel circuit question to get the full working, or combine any number of resistors, capacitors or inductors.</p><h2>Quick total for any components</h2><p>Assumes the inductors are not magnetically coupled (no shared magnetic field).</p><h2>Resistors in series and parallel</h2><p>In a series circuit the same current flows through every resistor, so their resistances simply add: R total = R1 + R2 + R3. In a parallel circuit every resistor has the same voltage across it and the current splits, so the reciprocals add: 1/R total = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3.</p><p>For exactly two resistors in parallel there is a shortcut, product over sum: R = (R1 × R2) ÷ (R1 + R2). For example 100 Ω and 220 Ω in parallel give 22,000 ÷ 320 = 68.75 Ω, and in series they give 320 Ω.</p><h2>Worked example: resistors in series</h2><p>A 24 V supply is connected to 4 Ω and 8 Ω resistors in series. Find the current and the voltage across each resistor.</p><ul><li>Total R = 4 + 8 = 12 Ω</li><li>V across 4 Ω = 2 × 4 = 8 V</li><li>V across 8 Ω = 2 × 8 = 16 V (check: 8 + 16 = 24 V)</li></ul><p>In series the current is the same everywhere and the supply voltage is shared, so the voltages across the resistors always add up to the supply voltage.</p><h2>Worked example: resistors in parallel</h2><p>A 12 V supply is connected to 6 Ω and 3 Ω resistors in parallel. Find the total current.</p><ul><li>Current through 6 Ω = 12 ÷ 6 = 2 A and through 3 Ω = 12 ÷ 3 = 4 A (check: 2 + 4 = 6 A)</li></ul><p>In parallel every resistor has the full supply voltage across it and the currents through the branches add up to the total current.</p><h2>Cells with internal resistance</h2><p>A real cell has some resistance of its own, called its internal resistance r. The current is I = E ÷ (R + r), where E is the emf. The voltage across the external resistors, the terminal p.d., is V = I × R, and the difference E − V = I × r is called the lost volts. For a 12 V cell with r = 1 Ω driving 11 Ω, I = 12 ÷ 12 = 1 A, the terminal p.d. is 11 V and 1 V is lost inside the cell.</p><h2>Capacitors in series and parallel</h2><p>Capacitors follow the opposite rules. In parallel their capacitances add: C total = C1 + C2. In series the reciprocals add: 1/C total = 1/C1 + 1/C2, so two 10 µF capacitors in series give 5 µF. Series capacitors share the voltage, which is why they are sometimes used to reach a higher voltage rating.</p><h2>Inductors in series and parallel</h2><p>Inductors that are far enough apart not to affect each other combine like resistors: they add in series and their reciprocals add in parallel. Coupled inductors, such as two windings on one core, need the mutual inductance as well, which this calculator does not include.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Need a single value? Read it with the Resistor Colour Code Calculator or the Capacitor Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/voltage-divider-calculator": "<main><h1>Voltage Divider Calculator</h1><p>Work out the output of a two-resistor voltage divider, or choose the resistor that gives the voltage you need.</p><p>Circuit: Vin → R1 → Vout → R2 → ground (0 V).</p><h2>The voltage divider formula</h2><p>A voltage divider (also called a potential divider) is two resistors in series across a supply. The output is taken from the point between them. Because the same current flows through both resistors, the supply voltage is shared in proportion to their resistances: Vout = Vin × R2 ÷ (R1 + R2).</p><h2>Worked example</h2><p>With Vin = 12 V, R1 = 10 kΩ and R2 = 4.7 kΩ, the output is 12 × 4,700 ÷ 14,700 = 3.84 V. The current through both resistors is 12 ÷ 14,700 = 0.816 mA, so each resistor dissipates well under a milliwatt.</p><p>To get 5 V from 12 V with R1 = 10 kΩ, rearrange the formula: R2 = Vout × R1 ÷ (Vin − Vout) = 5 × 10,000 ÷ 7 = 7.14 kΩ. The nearest E24 resistor is 7.5 kΩ, which gives 5.14 V.</p><h2>Where voltage dividers are used</h2><ul><li>Scaling a battery or sensor voltage down for a microcontroller's analogue input.</li><li>Setting the bias voltage of a transistor amplifier.</li><li>Reading light-dependent resistors and thermistors, which form one half of the divider.</li><li>Setting the output voltage of adjustable regulators such as the LM317.</li></ul><p>A potentiometer is a voltage divider in a single part: its wiper splits the track into R1 and R2.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Ohm's Law Calculator and Series and Parallel Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/led-resistor-calculator": "<main><h1>LED Resistor Calculator</h1><p>Find the current-limiting resistor for your LEDs, the standard value to buy, and the power rating it needs.</p><h2>LED resistor formula</h2><p>The resistor has to drop the voltage that the LEDs do not use. With n LEDs in series, R = (Vs − n × Vf) ÷ I, where Vs is the supply voltage, Vf is the forward voltage of one LED and I is the LED current in amps. The resistor's power is P = I² × R.</p><h2>Worked example: red LED on a 9 V battery</h2><p>A red LED with a forward voltage of 2 V should run at 20 mA. The resistor must drop 9 − 2 = 7 V, so R = 7 ÷ 0.02 = 350 Ω. The next E12 value up is 390 Ω, which gives a current of 7 ÷ 390 = 17.9 mA. The resistor dissipates about 0.126 W, so a 0.5 W resistor gives a safe margin (a 0.25 W part would run close to its limit).</p><h2>Typical LED forward voltages</h2><ul><li>Red and orange: about 1.8 to 2.2 V</li><li>Yellow and standard green: about 2.0 to 2.4 V</li><li>Blue, white and bright green: about 2.8 to 3.4 V</li><li>Infrared: about 1.2 to 1.5 V</li></ul><p>These are typical figures. The exact forward voltage depends on the LED and the current, so use the datasheet value when you have it.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Related tools: Ohm's Law Calculator and Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/engineering-tools/555-timer-calculator": "<main><h1>555 Timer Calculator</h1><p>Calculate the frequency and duty cycle of a 555 astable oscillator, design one for a target frequency, or find a monostable pulse width.</p><p>If the resistors fall outside 1 kΩ – 1 MΩ, choose a different capacitor and calculate again.</p><p>t = 1.1 × R × C. The output goes high for this long after each trigger.</p><h2>How the 555 timer works</h2><p>The 555 is one of the most popular integrated circuits ever made. Inside it are two comparators, a flip-flop and a discharge transistor. A timing capacitor charges through resistors, and the comparators switch the output when the capacitor voltage reaches two-thirds and one-third of the supply voltage. Because both thresholds are fractions of the supply, the timing hardly changes when the supply voltage changes.</p><h2>Astable mode formulas</h2><p>In astable mode the 555 runs continuously and produces a square wave. The capacitor charges through R1 and R2 and discharges through R2 only.</p><ul><li>Time high: t high = 0.693 × (R1 + R2) × C</li><li>Time low: t low = 0.693 × R2 × C</li><li>Frequency: f = 1 ÷ (0.693 × (R1 + 2R2) × C), about 1.44 ÷ ((R1 + 2R2) × C)</li><li>Duty cycle: (R1 + R2) ÷ (R1 + 2R2) × 100%</li></ul><p>Example: R1 = 1 kΩ, R2 = 10 kΩ and C = 1 µF give a frequency of about 68.7 Hz and a duty cycle of 52.4%. The number 0.693 is the natural logarithm of 2, which this calculator uses exactly.</p><h2>Monostable mode</h2><p>In monostable mode the 555 waits for a trigger pulse, then holds its output high for a fixed time t = 1.1 × R × C before returning low. It is used for timers, switch debouncing and delay circuits. For example, R = 100 kΩ and C = 10 µF give a pulse of 1.1 seconds.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><p>Read your parts with the Capacitor Code Calculator and the Resistor Colour Code Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/study-planner": "<main><h1>Study Planner for Students</h1><p>Your study plans automatically expire after 7 days</p><p>No study tasks yet</p><h2>What is a Study Planner?</h2><p>A study planner helps students organize their daily academic tasks, manage time effectively, and improve productivity.</p><h2>Why You Need a Study Planner</h2><ul><li>Stay organized with your study schedule</li><li>Avoid last-minute cramming</li><li>Improve academic performance</li><li>Manage your time effectively</li><li>Receive automatic study reminders</li></ul><h2>How This Planner Works</h2><p>Add your study tasks with a date and time. StudentToolsNG will remind you using browser notifications and alarm sounds.</p><h2>Important Notice</h2><p>Study tasks automatically expire after 7 days to help students stay active and organized.</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator and JAMB Score Calculator .</p><h2>// Smart Study Planner //</h2><p>// Your study plans automatically expire // after 7 days //</p><p>// No study plans yet //</p><h2>// What is a Study Planner? //</h2><p>// A study planner helps students // organize their academic tasks, // manage time effectively, // and improve productivity. //</p><h2>// Why Use This Planner? //</h2><ul><li>// Improve focus and consistency //</li><li>// Avoid procrastination //</li><li>// Track daily study sessions //</li><li>// Build better study habits //</li><li>// Plans auto-delete after 7 days //</li></ul><h2>// Student Productivity Tips //</h2><p>// Study difficult subjects first, // revise consistently, // and create achievable daily goals. //</p><p>// You can also use our // // CGPA Calculator // // and // // JAMB Score Calculator // //</p><h2>Study Planner for Students</h2><h2>What is a Study Planner?</h2><p>// A study planner helps students organize their daily academic tasks, // manage time effectively, and improve productivity. //</p><h2>Why You Need a Study Planner</h2><ul><li>Stay organized with your subjects</li><li>Avoid last-minute cramming</li><li>Improve academic performance</li><li>Manage your time better</li></ul><h2>How to Use This Study Planner</h2><p>// Add your daily study tasks above and track your progress. You can // delete tasks once completed. //</p><h2>Study Tips for Nigerian Students</h2><p>// Create a daily routine, focus on difficult subjects first, and // use tools like CGPA calculators to track your progress. //</p><p>// You can also use our // CGPA Calculator // and // JAMB Score Calculator. //</p></main>",
 "/scholarships": "<main><h1>Scholarship Opportunities</h1><p>Learn about eligibility, application process, and deadlines for this scholarship.</p><h2>Scholarships for Nigerian Students</h2><p>Scholarships help Nigerian students reduce the cost of education and achieve their academic goals without financial stress. Many organizations including government bodies and private companies offer scholarships yearly.</p><h2>Types of Scholarships Available</h2><ul><li>Undergraduate scholarships</li><li>Postgraduate scholarships</li><li>Merit-based scholarships</li><li>Need-based scholarships</li></ul><h2>How to Apply for Scholarships</h2><p>To apply for scholarships in Nigeria, students should monitor deadlines, prepare required documents, and apply through official portals.</p><h2>Who Offers Scholarships in Nigeria?</h2><ul><li>Federal and state governments: through scholarship boards and education trust funds, often for undergraduates in Nigerian universities and for study abroad in priority courses.</li><li>Companies: especially oil and gas, banking and telecoms firms, many of which run yearly undergraduate awards for students in their host communities or in particular courses.</li><li>Foundations and NGOs: private and religious foundations that support students with financial need or outstanding results.</li><li>Universities: internal awards for the best students in a faculty or department.</li><li>Foreign governments and international bodies: for example the Commonwealth and Chevening scholarships for postgraduate study in the United Kingdom.</li></ul><h2>Common Eligibility Requirements</h2><ul><li>Admission into, or current study at, an accredited institution.</li><li>A minimum academic standard, such as five O-level credits including English and Mathematics, a minimum JAMB score, or a minimum CGPA (often 3.0 or 3.5 on a 5-point scale).</li><li>A particular course of study, level, state of origin or community.</li><li>Proof of financial need for need-based awards.</li><li>For postgraduate and international awards: a good first degree, work experience and evidence of leadership.</li></ul><p>Not sure where you stand? Use our CGPA Calculator and GPA Class Calculator to check your result against the minimum required before you apply.</p><h2>Documents You Usually Need</h2><ul><li>Birth certificate or declaration of age, and a valid means of identification (NIN slip, passport).</li><li>O-level results (WAEC or NECO) and JAMB result slip.</li><li>University admission letter and current student ID card.</li><li>Academic transcript or statement of result showing your CGPA.</li><li>Local government or state of origin certificate, where required.</li><li>Passport photographs.</li><li>A personal statement or essay, and reference letters for many awards.</li></ul><p>Scan these documents clearly in advance so you can apply quickly when a scholarship opens.</p><h2>Tips for a Strong Application</h2><ul><li>Start early: many scholarships open and close within a few weeks each year.</li><li>Read the requirements carefully and submit every document asked for; incomplete applications are often rejected automatically.</li><li>Write your own essay: explain your goals, your challenges, what you have achieved and how the award will help you. Be specific and honest.</li><li>Choose referees who know you well, such as a lecturer or principal, and give them enough time.</li><li>Prepare for aptitude tests and interviews: many company scholarships include an online test in verbal and numerical reasoning.</li><li>Keep your grades up: most awards require you to maintain a minimum CGPA to keep the scholarship.</li></ul><h2>Avoid Scholarship Scams</h2><ul><li>Genuine scholarships do not ask you to pay a fee to receive an award.</li><li>Apply only through the official website or portal of the organisation offering the scholarship.</li><li>Be wary of messages promising guaranteed awards or asking for your bank PIN or BVN.</li><li>Check announcements on the organisation's official website or verified social media pages.</li></ul><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator to track your academic performance while applying for scholarships.</p></main>",
 "/admission-predictor": "<main><h1>Admission Chance Calculator Nigeria</h1><h2>How Admission is Calculated in Nigeria</h2><p>Most Nigerian universities use a combination of JAMB and Post UTME scores to determine admission eligibility. Each school has its own formula.</p><h2>How This Admission Predictor Works</h2><p>This tool estimates your admission chances using a weighted formula: 60% JAMB score and 40% Post UTME score.</p><h2>Example Calculation</h2><p>If you score 250 in JAMB and 70 in Post UTME:</p><h2>Admission Chance Guide</h2><ul><li>70+ → High Admission Chance</li><li>60–69 → Moderate Chance</li><li>Below 60 → Low Chance</li></ul><h2>Important Note</h2><p>Admission depends on your course, school cut-off mark, and competition level. This tool provides an estimate, not a guarantee.</p><p>You can also calculate your JAMB Score and CGPA.</p></main>",
 "/ai-tutor": "<main><h1>AI Tutor for Students</h1><h3>Answer</h3><h2>What is an AI Tutor?</h2><p>An AI tutor is an intelligent learning assistant that helps students solve problems, understand concepts, and get instant answers to academic questions.</p><h2>How This AI Tutor Helps You</h2><ul><li>Answer academic questions instantly</li><li>Explain difficult topics</li><li>Help with assignments and homework</li><li>Support learning in mathematics, science, and more</li></ul><h2>How to Use the AI Tutor</h2><p>Type your question in the input box above and click \"Ask AI\". The system will generate a helpful answer instantly.</p><h2>Example Questions You Can Ask</h2><p>What is CGPA?</p><p>Solve 2x + 4 = 10</p><p>Explain WAEC grading system</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/tutorials/math-calculator": "<main><h1>Smart Math Calculator</h1><h2>How to Use the Smart Math Calculator</h2><p>Type a maths question in plain words or numbers, then press Solve. The calculator works out the answer and shows the steps, so you can follow the method and use it in your own WAEC, NECO, JAMB or class exercises. You can also tap any of the example chips above the input box to try a question instantly.</p><ul><li>Type the question, for example 2x + 4 = 10 or 20% of 150.</li><li>Use / for fractions (3/4), ^ for powers (2^3) and : for ratios (2:3).</li><li>For two equations, separate them with \"and\" or a comma.</li><li>Read the steps, not just the final answer, so you learn the method.</li></ul><h2>Topics the Calculator Can Solve</h2><ul><li>Algebra: linear equations such as 3x − 5 = 16 and x/2 + 3 = 7.</li><li>Simultaneous equations: pairs such as 2x + y = 5 and x − y = 1.</li><li>Fractions: adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing.</li><li>Percentages: percentage of a quantity, percentage increase and decrease, reverse percentages.</li><li>Ratio and proportion: sharing a quantity in a given ratio.</li><li>Simple interest: from principal (p), rate (r) and time (t).</li><li>Profit and loss: from cost price and selling price, including profit percent.</li><li>Speed, distance and time.</li><li>Averages, indices, sets (Venn diagram counts), area and perimeter, age and mixture problems.</li><li>Physics: force from mass and acceleration (F = ma).</li></ul><h2>Worked Examples</h2><h3>1. Solve 2x + 4 = 10</h3><p>Subtract 4 from both sides: 2x = 6. Divide both sides by 2: x = 3. Check: 2 × 3 + 4 = 10.</p><h3>2. Add 1/2 + 3/4</h3><p>The lowest common denominator is 4. 1/2 = 2/4, so 2/4 + 3/4 = 5/4 = 1 1/4.</p><h3>3. Divide ₦100 000 in the ratio 2 : 3</h3><p>Total parts = 2 + 3 = 5. One part = 100 000 ÷ 5 = 20 000. Shares: 2 × 20 000 = ₦40 000 and 3 × 20 000 = ₦60 000.</p><h3>4. Simple interest on ₦1000 at 5% for 2 years</h3><p>I = (P × R × T) ÷ 100 = (1000 × 5 × 2) ÷ 100 = ₦100. Amount = 1000 + 100 = ₦1100.</p><h3>5. Solve 2x + y = 5 and x − y = 1</h3><p>Add the equations to eliminate y: 3x = 6, so x = 2. Substitute into x − y = 1: 2 − y = 1, so y = 1. Answer: x = 2, y = 1.</p><h3>6. A trader buys goods for ₦400 and sells them for ₦500</h3><p>Profit = 500 − 400 = ₦100. Profit percent = (100 ÷ 400) × 100 = 25%.</p><h2>Tips for Using a Math Solver Well</h2><ul><li>Try the question yourself first, then use the calculator to check your answer.</li><li>If your answer differs, compare each step to find exactly where the mistake happened.</li><li>In examinations you must show your working, so copy the method, not just the answer.</li><li>Practise similar questions in our practice quiz and read the step-by-step mathematics tutorials.</li></ul></main>",
 "/quiz": "<main><h2>🧠 Quiz Mode</h2><h2>Practice Mathematics with Interactive Quiz</h2><p>This math quiz helps students practice key topics such as algebra, fractions, percentages, ratios, and simple interest. Each question is generated dynamically to improve problem-solving skills and speed.</p><h3>Topics Covered</h3><ul><li>Algebra equations</li><li>Fractions and ratios</li><li>Percentages</li><li>Simple interest</li><li>Set theory basics</li></ul><h3>Why Use This Quiz?</h3><p>Practicing regularly helps students build confidence and improve accuracy in exams like WAEC and JAMB. The leaderboard feature also allows students to compete and track their performance.</p><h3>Related Tools</h3><ul><li>Math Solver</li><li>CGPA Calculator</li><li>JAMB Calculator</li></ul><h3>🏆 What should we call you?</h3><p>Enter a name so your score shows up correctly on the leaderboard — no email or password needed.</p></main>",
 "/marketplace": "<main><h1>Research Library</h1><p>Project materials, research documents and study guides from independent publishers. Read chapters 1 &amp; 2 free on every document — unlock the full work when it's what you need.</p><h2>How the Research Library Works</h2><ul><li>Search or browse by keyword or field of study to find project guides and research documents.</li><li>Read the free preview: chapters 1 and 2 of every document are open to read, so you can judge its quality and relevance before paying.</li><li>Unlock the full document with a secure online payment if it is what you need. It is then available under My Purchases.</li></ul><h2>Using Research Materials Responsibly</h2><p>Documents in the library are guides to help you plan and write your own work: how to frame a research problem, review literature, choose a methodology, design a questionnaire and present results. Use them to learn the structure and approach, collect your own data, and always cite any source you quote or adapt. Submitting someone else's work as your own is plagiarism and can lead to serious academic penalties.</p><h2>Have research or project work to sell?</h2><p>Publish it here, set your price, and get paid straight to your bank account every time someone unlocks it.</p></main>",
 "/about": "<main><h1>About StudentToolsNG</h1><p>StudentToolsNG is an educational platform designed to help students easily calculate their academic results and access helpful learning resources.</p><p>Our platform provides free tools such as CGPA calculators, WAEC grade calculators, and JAMB score calculators to simplify academic planning for Nigerian students.</p><p>In addition to academic tools, StudentToolsNG also provides tutorials, study guides, and scholarship opportunities to help students succeed in school.</p><h2>Our Mission</h2><p>Our mission is to make academic tools and educational resources easily accessible to every Nigerian student.</p><h2>What We Offer</h2><ul><li>CGPA Calculator for university students</li><li>WAEC grade calculator</li><li>JAMB score calculator</li><li>Study tutorials and guides</li><li>Scholarship opportunities</li><li>Study planning tools</li></ul><h2>Contact Us</h2><p>If you have questions, suggestions, or feedback, feel free to reach out through our contact page.</p><h2>Who We Are</h2><p>StudentToolsNG was created to solve real problems faced by Nigerian students when calculating CGPA, understanding WAEC grading, and preparing for JAMB.</p><p>We understand the challenges students face, and we are committed to providing simple, accurate, and reliable academic tools.</p><p>You can explore our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator to get started.</p><h2>Why Choose StudentToolsNG?</h2><ul><li>100% Free academic tools</li><li>Designed specifically for Nigerian students</li><li>Simple and easy to use</li><li>Accurate and reliable calculations</li></ul></main>",
 "/contact": "<main><h1>Contact Us</h1><h2>Get in Touch with StudentToolsNG</h2><p>Do you have questions about our CGPA calculator, WAEC grading system, or JAMB score tools? We are here to help Nigerian students succeed academically.</p><h2>Why Contact Us?</h2><ul><li>Get help using our academic tools</li><li>Report bugs or issues</li><li>Request new features</li><li>Partnership and collaboration enquiries</li></ul><h2>Support for Nigerian Students</h2><p>StudentToolsNG provides free tools and tutorials designed specifically for Nigerian universities, WAEC, and JAMB students.</p><p>You can also explore our CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p><h2>What to Include in Your Message</h2><ul><li>The page or tool you were using, for example the CGPA calculator or a particular tutorial.</li><li>What you expected to happen and what actually happened.</li><li>The device and browser you used, if you are reporting a problem.</li><li>For a tutorial request, the subject, topic and level (WAEC, JAMB or university).</li></ul><p>Clear details help us answer you faster and fix problems for every student.</p><h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2><h3>Are the tools on StudentToolsNG free?</h3><p>Yes. The CGPA, WAEC, JAMB and GPA class calculators, the study planner, the math calculator and the tutorials are free to use.</p><h3>How is my CGPA calculated?</h3><p>Each course's grade point is multiplied by its credit units, the results are added together, and the total is divided by the total units. Our CGPA Calculator does this for you and explains how CGPA works.</p><h3>Can I suggest a tutorial topic?</h3><p>Yes. Send us the subject and topic using the form above. We publish new physics, mathematics, biology, chemistry and programming tutorials regularly.</p><h3>Can I sell my research or project materials?</h3><p>Yes. Publishers can list research documents in our Research Library and set their own price.</p><h3>How is my information used?</h3><p>Please read our Privacy Policy for details of the information we collect and how it is used.</p></main>",
 "/privacy-policy": "<main><h1>Privacy Policy</h1><p>This Privacy Policy explains how StudentToolsNG collects, uses, and protects information when you use our website.</p><h2>1. Information We Collect</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may collect limited information such as:</p><ul><li>Name and email submitted through contact forms</li><li>Usage data such as pages visited</li><li>Device and browser information</li></ul><h2>2. How We Use Your Information</h2><p>We use the information collected to:</p><ul><li>Improve our academic tools</li><li>Respond to user inquiries</li><li>Analyze website performance</li><li>Provide better learning resources</li></ul><h2>3. Cookies</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may use cookies to improve user experience, analyze traffic, and remember user preferences.</p><h2>4. Third-Party Services</h2><p>We may use third-party services such as analytics providers and hosting platforms to improve the performance of the website.</p><h2>4a. Advertising (Google AdSense)</h2><p>StudentToolsNG uses Google AdSense to display advertisements. Google, as a third-party vendor, uses cookies to serve ads based on your prior visits to this website or other websites. Google's use of advertising cookies enables it and its partners to serve ads based on your visit to this site and/or other sites on the internet.</p><p>You may opt out of personalized advertising by visiting Google Ads Settings . Alternatively, you can opt out of a third-party vendor's use of cookies for personalized advertising by visiting www.aboutads.info .</p><h2>5. External Links</h2><p>Our website may contain links to external websites. We are not responsible for the privacy practices of those websites.</p><h2>6. Data Security</h2><p>We take reasonable steps to protect user information from unauthorized access or disclosure.</p><h2>7. Children's Information</h2><p>StudentToolsNG is designed for educational purposes. We do not knowingly collect personal data from children without parental consent.</p><h2>8. Changes to This Policy</h2><p>We may update this privacy policy from time to time. Updates will be posted on this page.</p><h2>9. Contact Us</h2><p>If you have questions about this Privacy Policy, please contact us through the contact page.</p><p>Explore more tools: You can also check our WAEC Calculator and JAMB Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/terms": "<main><h1>Terms and Conditions for StudentToolsNG</h1><p>Welcome to StudentToolsNG. By accessing and using this website, you agree to comply with the following terms and conditions.</p><h2>Use of Our Tools</h2><p>StudentToolsNG provides academic tools such as CGPA calculators, WAEC grade calculators, and JAMB score calculators for educational purposes only.</p><p>While we strive to provide accurate calculations, users should verify their academic results with their respective institutions.</p><h2>Educational Content</h2><p>Tutorials and educational materials published on StudentToolsNG are intended to support students in their learning journey.</p><p>We do not guarantee academic outcomes based on the use of our tools or tutorials.</p><h2>User Responsibility</h2><p>Users are responsible for ensuring the accuracy of the information they enter into any calculators or tools on this website.</p><h2>External Links</h2><p>StudentToolsNG may contain links to external websites. We are not responsible for the content or practices of these external sites.</p><h2>Changes to These Terms</h2><p>We reserve the right to update these terms at any time. Users are encouraged to review this page periodically.</p><h2>Contact</h2><p>If you have questions about these Terms and Conditions, please contact us through the contact page.</p><p>You can also use our CGPA Calculator and JAMB Score Calculator.</p></main>",
 "/author": "<main><h1>Engr. Henry Akpan</h1><p>Electrical and Electronics Engineer, Full-Stack Web Developer and Mathematics &amp; Physics Tutor. Founder of StudentToolsNG.</p><p>Passionate about building technology solutions that help students succeed academically.</p><h2>About the Author</h2><p>Engr. Henry Akpan is an Electrical Engineer and Full-Stack Developer with a passion for building educational tools that simplify learning for Nigerian students.</p><p>He is the founder of StudentToolsNG, a platform that provides free academic calculators, tutorials, and study resources.</p><h2>Skills &amp; Expertise</h2><ul><li>Full-Stack Web Development (React, Node.js)</li><li>Electrical and Electronics Engineer</li><li>UI UX Designer</li><li>Mathematics and Physics Tutoring</li><li>Educational Technology Solutions</li></ul><p>Explore tools created by Henry such as the CGPA Calculator, WAEC Calculator, and JAMB Calculator.</p><h2>Articles by Henry Akpan</h2><ul><li>How to Calculate CGPA</li><li>WAEC Grading System</li></ul></main>"
};
